<h2><strong>FAQ On Back-to-School Eye Exam</strong></h2>
<h3><strong>Does my child need an eye exam if they passed the school vision screening? </strong></h3>
<p>Yes. School screenings only check basic distance vision. A comprehensive exam looks at how the eyes work together, how they track across text, how they focus, and the overall health of the eye. A child can pass a school screening and still have a vision problem that&#8217;s slowing them down in class.</p>
<h3><strong>At what age should kids get their first eye exam? </strong></h3>
<p>The American Optometric Association recommends the first exam by 6 months of age, then again at age 3, and once more before starting school at around age 5. After that, every one to two years, depending on what the exam finds.</p>
<h3><strong>What are the signs my child might have a vision problem? </strong></h3>
<p>Squinting, tilting the head to look at something, losing their place while reading, avoiding books, sitting too close to the TV, frequent headaches after homework, or rubbing their eyes a lot. The tricky part is that children often don&#8217;t say anything because they don&#8217;t know what a clear vision is supposed to feel like.</p>
<h3><strong>Can a vision problem be mistaken for ADHD or a learning disability? </strong></h3>
<p>Yes, and it happens more often than most parents realize. When a child can&#8217;t see clearly or their eyes aren&#8217;t working together properly, they get distracted, frustrated, and disengaged. That pattern looks a lot like attention issues. A proper eye exam is a reasonable first step before any referral for behavioral evaluation.</p>
<h3><strong>What&#8217;s the difference between an optometrist and an optician? </strong></h3>
<p>An optometrist is a doctor who examines the eyes, diagnoses conditions, and writes prescriptions. An optician is a licensed specialist who fills those prescriptions and fits glasses or contact lenses. At Austin Optics, Dr. Anushka Mistry handles the exam side, and Iris brings 30 years of fitting experience to the eyewear side. Both matter, and having them under one roof makes the whole process easier for families.</p>
<h3><strong>How long does a children&#8217;s eye exam take? </strong></h3>
<p>Usually 30 to 45 minutes. It depends on the child&#8217;s age and whether any follow-up testing is needed. Younger children may take a little longer since the process is adapted to keep them comfortable.</p>
<h3><strong>My child is scared of eye doctors. What should I do? </strong></h3>
<p>It helps to tell them what to expect beforehand: the doctor will show them pictures or letters, shine a small light near their eyes, and ask them questions. Nothing hurts. Dr. Mistry works with children regularly and takes the time to ease them in before the exam starts. If it helps, you can call us ahead of the visit, and we&#8217;ll walk you through how to prepare your child.</p>
